     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, because the dose looks unsafe.\n\nIbuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg per day. That's twice the usual maximum for hospitalized adults, which is 3,200 mg per day. A dose that high raises the risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nOrders like this are normally placed by a licensed prescriber. If the treating clinician meant a different dose, one of these may be what they intended:\n- **400 mg by mouth four times daily** (1,600 mg per day)\n- **800 mg by mouth three times daily** (2,400 mg per day, a common prescription maximum)\n- **600 mg by mouth four times daily** (2,400 mg per day)\n\nIf you or the treating clinician confirm the intended dose, I can enter it right away. If the 1600 mg dose is intentional, the prescribing clinician should confirm it and add a note with their rationale. I can include that note on the order."
      }
     ],
